# ripgrep global config — loaded because $RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH points here
# (set in zsh/.zshrc_config.zsh). Every `rg` invocation reads this file,
# INCLUDING the ones telescope and the snacks nvim picker shell out to. So
# keep it to `--type-add` lines only: those merely *register* file types and
# change no search behavior. Do NOT add flags like --smart-case or --hidden
# here — they would silently apply to every editor picker search too.
#
# Type globs are matched against the file NAME only; directory components are
# ignored. So dir-based conventions can't be captured here (Rust's tests/,
# Go's testdata/), and Rust's inline #[cfg(test)] unit tests aren't a separate
# file at all — rusttest below only catches the *_test.rs naming some projects
# use. List all built-in types with:  rg --type-list

# --- Aliases for awkwardly-named built-in types --------------------------
# ripgrep's Rust type is `rust`, not `rs`; add the extension-shaped alias so
# `-trs` works like the built-in `-tgo` / `-tlua` / `-tpy` already do.
--type-add=rs:*.rs

# --- Per-language test-file types ----------------------------------------
# Each matches the conventional test-file NAMING for a language we configure
# an LSP/formatter for in nvim. Use `-t<lang>test` to search only tests, or
# `-T<lang>test` to exclude them from a search.
--type-add=gotest:*_test.go
--type-add=rusttest:*_test.rs
--type-add=pytest:{test_*,*_test}.py
--type-add=jstest:*.{test,spec}.{js,jsx,cjs,mjs}
--type-add=tstest:*.{test,spec}.{ts,tsx,cts,mts}
--type-add=extest:*_test.exs
--type-add=kttest:*{Test,Tests}.kt
--type-add=luatest:*_{spec,test}.lua

# --- Umbrella `test` type ------------------------------------------------
# Union of every per-language test type above (ripgrep's `include:` composes
# already-defined types, so these lines must come after them). `-Ttest` drops
# all test files from a search in one flag; `-ttest` searches only tests
# across every language.
--type-add=test:include:gotest,rusttest,pytest,jstest,tstest,extest,kttest,luatest
